From u2b27719db7267c87 at groups.google.com  Fri Sep 13 23:47:50 2013
From: u2b27719db7267c87 at groups.google.com (Bonnie Dixon)
Date: Fri, 13 Sep 2013 23:47:50 +0000
Subject: bracket indexing with a logical expression
Message-ID: <sGu-otlbAMo.gJvZwzTgl00J@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/sGu-otlbAMo/m/gJvZwzTgl00J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi D-RUG folks.

I have noticed something odd when using bracket indexing with a logical expression to select a subset of elements, and I am wondering if anyone can explain this behavior.

If there are missing values in a vector, then these seem to be included when selecting a subset this way.

> x <- c(5, NA, 7, 2, 3, NA, 8)
> x
[1]  5 NA  7  2  3 NA  8
> x < 4
[1] FALSE    NA FALSE  TRUE  TRUE    NA FALSE
> x[x < 4]
[1] NA  2  3 NA
> x[x == 3]
[1] NA  3 NA

However, when reassigning the selected subset of elements to a different value, the missing values do not seem to "take" the reassignment.

> x[x < 4] <- 0
> x
[1]  5 NA  7  0  0 NA  8

Is this the expected behavior?  It seems inconsistent.

Thanks.

Bonnie

From ub35e99045f6c74c2 at groups.google.com  Sat Sep 14 02:33:12 2013
From: ub35e99045f6c74c2 at groups.google.com (Vince S. Buffalo)
Date: Sat, 14 Sep 2013 02:33:12 +0000
Subject: Re: [D-RUG]: bracket indexing with a logical expression
Message-ID: <sGu-otlbAMo.NIw97q8h20kJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <sGu-otlbAMo.gJvZwzTgl00J@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
References: <sGu-otlbAMo.gJvZwzTgl00J@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/sGu-otlbAMo/m/NIw97q8h20kJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Bonnie,

This is expected, and a rather nice feature of R — that it truly understands how to handle missing data. Since we don't know the true values of these elements, R will list them (since they *could* be less than four), but doesn't change them (since if they are less than four in the assignment is unknown). It's quite nice, even though the behavior is admittedly a bit strange at first.

Vince

